WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.056
So I don't work on refugees

00:00:02.056 --> 00:00:03.976
specifically i work on migration writ large

00:00:03.976 --> 00:00:07.695
My contribution here tonight

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:09.885
is maybe to frame this question

00:00:09.885 --> 00:00:12.736
around what we understand the refugee

00:00:12.736 --> 00:00:15.256
crisis to be. And how we define or

00:00:15.256 --> 00:00:16.906
distinguish rather refugees from

00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:19.906
migrants. So the concept of a refugee is

00:00:19.906 --> 00:00:22.545
actually a legal concept that emerged in

00:00:22.545 --> 00:00:25.486
the immediate post-war years.

00:00:25.486 --> 00:00:30.106
The convention was ratified in 1951

00:00:30.106 --> 00:00:32.356
It is a distinction that grew out of the

00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:34.606
failure of European countries primarily

00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:37.245
to deal with the World War II refugees

00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:39.975
the massive movement of people who had

00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:43.006
been displaced by this cataclysmic event.

00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:46.036
This is the distinction that we still use.

00:00:46.036 --> 00:00:50.745
And we haven't deployed it or

00:00:50.745 --> 00:00:52.725
thought through it as deliberately as

00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:54.556
we've been thinking about it over the

00:00:54.556 --> 00:00:57.856
past five years because the major

00:00:57.856 --> 00:01:00.346
refugee crisis or the major forced

00:01:00.346 --> 00:01:03.226
displacements of people have been in

00:01:03.226 --> 00:01:05.026
areas that have received far less

00:01:05.026 --> 00:01:07.395
coverage in the US and in Europe--so

00:01:07.395 --> 00:01:09.946
primarily sub-Saharan Africa. and with

00:01:09.946 --> 00:01:12.166
internal displacement in sub-Saharan Africa

00:01:12.166 --> 00:01:15.285
and Latin America--Columbia primarily.

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:17.956
Columbia has a huge internally

00:01:17.956 --> 00:01:20.566
displaced persons problem. In fact it was

00:01:20.566 --> 00:01:24.825
interesting in your map that the causes

00:01:24.825 --> 00:01:26.505
for displacement we're all natural

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:29.446
disasters but in fact in Colombia

00:01:29.446 --> 00:01:31.486
the major cause of displacement is

00:01:31.486 --> 00:01:35.086
actually violence and civil war.

00:01:35.086 --> 00:01:39.376
So my understanding is that it might

00:01:39.376 --> 00:01:41.116
be helpful to present a little bit of

00:01:41.116 --> 00:01:42.556
the "long now"

00:01:42.556 --> 00:01:45.556
Yes would that be helpful? That's always welcome.

00:01:45.556 --> 00:01:49.366
Right, so this notion (refugee status) is a notion

00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:51.166
that emerged right after

00:01:51.166 --> 00:01:55.096
after World  War II. And for the US,

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:57.616
the North Atlantic region, and Europe

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:00.855
migration has been for the most part this

00:02:00.855 --> 00:02:08.146
steady regulated flow of people.There have been labor movement

00:02:08.146 --> 00:02:11.536
accords. So Europe imported

00:02:11.536 --> 00:02:13.276
guest workers from North Africa, from

00:02:13.276 --> 00:02:16.216
Turkey the US signed the Bracero program

00:02:16.216 --> 00:02:18.496
with Mexico. So these

00:02:18.496 --> 00:02:21.555
regulated flows that had informal

00:02:21.555 --> 00:02:24.106
undocumented components, but no big spikes.

00:02:24.106 --> 00:02:30.136
The big spike is what has drawn our

00:02:30.136 --> 00:02:32.716
attention--the big spike to Europe--has

00:02:32.716 --> 00:02:34.695
drawn our attention to the refugee

00:02:34.695 --> 00:02:37.996
crisis today. Lebanon and Turkey and

00:02:37.996 --> 00:02:40.516
Jordan have experienced these flows in

00:02:40.516 --> 00:02:43.365
big numbers really since the beginning

00:02:43.365 --> 00:02:47.356
of the Civil War. The US in

00:02:47.356 --> 00:02:51.106
particular has in its history--and I know

00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:52.786
there's an American historian in our

00:02:52.786 --> 00:02:54.826
audience here, so I may stand corrected--

00:02:54.826 --> 00:02:57.736
but in its history the US has

00:02:57.736 --> 00:03:02.055
experienced these big spikes and has

00:03:02.055 --> 00:03:04.665
not had the same kind of anxiety around

00:03:04.665 --> 00:03:06.796
these big spikes in the past. One

00:03:06.796 --> 00:03:09.585
example that for me is very poignant is

00:03:09.585 --> 00:03:13.126
the Irish potato famine. So Ireland

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:18.106
between 01847, which was

00:03:18.106 --> 00:03:21.736
the first year of the famine, and 01851

00:03:21.736 --> 00:03:31.246
lost 1.5 million people. It was a population in the

00:03:31.246 --> 00:03:36.406
last census before the famine

00:03:36.406 --> 00:03:38.746
listed the population of Ireland at 8 million.

00:03:38.746 --> 00:03:41.805
So it's an equivalent number to the

00:03:41.805 --> 00:03:44.716
kind of displacement proportionally that

00:03:44.716 --> 00:03:49.546
you see from Syria today. Most of,

00:03:49.546 --> 00:03:56.686
a huge number, of Irish ended up in New York, in Boston,

00:03:56.686 --> 00:03:59.445
in Philadelphia, and in Montreal, Toronto...

00:03:59.445 --> 00:04:02.115
They traveled across the Atlantic

00:04:02.115 --> 00:04:04.216
in slave ships that had been

00:04:04.216 --> 00:04:07.755
repurposed for the movement of Irish

00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:12.346
immigrants. And Manhattan which in 01847

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:15.466
had a population of 300,000 received

00:04:15.466 --> 00:04:19.336
200,000 Irish people in two years.

00:04:19.336 --> 00:04:21.886
Its population grew by 1/4.

00:04:21.886 --> 00:04:24.166
So did set the population of

00:04:24.166 --> 00:04:27.496
Boston, Philadelphia... one more city that

00:04:27.496 --> 00:04:30.436
I'm forgetting. Baltimore, maybe?

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.776
No, Chicago didn't get a lot of Irish.

00:04:32.776 --> 00:04:34.816
They got a lot of Germans who moved

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:39.676
to the Midwest. But these

00:04:39.676 --> 00:04:43.726
populations were not tagged as refugees.

00:04:43.726 --> 00:04:45.286
They were just seen as migrants. And the

00:04:45.286 --> 00:04:49.366
cities that received them absorbed them.

00:04:49.366 --> 00:04:51.706
We didn't tag them differently.

00:04:51.706 --> 00:04:54.825
The tagging of refugees as

00:04:54.825 --> 00:04:56.416
distinct from migrants is a

00:04:56.416 --> 00:05:02.385
new phenomenon. As is the sense that cities

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.846
in Europe and North America cannot

00:05:04.846 --> 00:05:09.496
absorb them. We have in the past and it

00:05:09.496 --> 00:05:11.446
wasn't as traumatic as one might think.

00:05:11.446 --> 00:05:13.816
It has shaped our understanding of things like

00:05:13.816 --> 00:05:15.885
infrastructure and poverty. But basically

00:05:15.885 --> 00:05:19.726
we have in the past had this

00:05:19.726 --> 00:05:22.186
capacity. And it's this new tagging that

00:05:22.186 --> 00:05:24.916
has transformed our vision of what we

00:05:24.916 --> 00:05:38.625
can absorb and what we can't absorb.

